@startuml
skinparam backgroundColor white
skinparam participantPadding 80
skinparam wrapWidth 0
skinparam maxMessageSize 500

participant "Master/\nOrchestrator" as Master
participant "SU1" as SU1
participant "SU2" as SU2
participant "SU3" as SU3

== Jacobi Co-Simulation: One Macro Step t_n -> t_(n+1) ==

Master -> Master: **Phase 1: Freeze All Inputs**
note right: Inputs use outputs available at t_n,\nproduced by the previous macro step.

Master -> SU1: set u1(t_n) from y(t_n)
SU1 --> Master: input set
Master -> SU2: set u2(t_n) from y(t_n)
SU2 --> Master: input set
Master -> SU3: set u3(t_n) from y(t_n)
SU3 --> Master: input set

Master -> Master: **Phase 2: Advance Independent Components**
note right: The current SysSimX implementation\ninvokes component steps serially.

group Serial component loop
    Master -> SU1: do_step(t_n, H)
    activate SU1
    SU1 -> SU1: advance x1 and update y1
    SU1 --> Master: y1(t_(n+1))
    deactivate SU1

    Master -> SU2: do_step(t_n, H)
    activate SU2
    SU2 -> SU2: advance x2 and update y2
    SU2 --> Master: y2(t_(n+1))
    deactivate SU2

    Master -> SU3: do_step(t_n, H)
    activate SU3
    SU3 -> SU3: advance x3 and update y3
    SU3 --> Master: y3(t_(n+1))
    deactivate SU3
end

note over Master, SU3
The frozen-input semantics make these advances mathematically independent
and candidates for future parallel execution; no concurrency is used today.
end note

Master -> Master: Exchange outputs for the next macro step
@enduml
